{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c427","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07721-ca06-7081-babf-b563190d8d78"},"sequence":110,"recorded_at":1780531400000215,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd"},"call_id":"call_01a07721ff0c75b0b065767a435da614","text":"Read text file `internal/secprim/nofollow.go`.\n1|package secprim\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"errors\"\n5|\t\"os\"\n6|)\n7|\n8|// errIsSymlink reports the Windows Lstat gate refusal. It stays internal:\n9|// callers see the failPath refusal, and errors.Is against ErrUnsafePath\n10|// is the supported match.\n11|var errIsSymlink = errors.New(\"secprim symlink refused before open\")\n12|\n13|// OpenNoFollowFile opens an existing regular file read-only, refusing a\n14|// trailing symlink instead of traversing it (Section 16.3\n15|// \"symlink/reparse escape during both validation and commit\"). The\n16|// descriptor refers to the opened inode, and the post-open Fstat below\n17|// reads that descriptor rather than the path, so a swap between open and\n18|// stat cannot retarget the check: only the final-component open itself\n19|// races, and on unix O_NOFOLLOW closes even that.\n20|//\n21|// The file must be regular: directories, devices, pipes, and sockets are\n22|// refused after opening (and the descriptor closed) rather than handed\n23|// back. Parent directories are the caller's responsibility: open the\n24|// staging root with OpenNoFollowDir and resolve members through a Guard.\n25|func OpenNoFollowFile(path string) (*os.File, error) {\n26|\tfile, err := openNoFollow(path)\n27|\tif err != nil {\n28|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n29|\t}\n30|\tinfo, err := statOpenedFile(file)\n31|\tif err != nil {\n32|\t\t_ = file.Close()\n33|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open stat failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n34|\t}\n35|\tif err := CheckRegularTarget(info); err != nil {\n36|\t\t_ = file.Close()\n37|\t\treturn nil, err\n38|\t}\n39|\treturn file, nil\n40|}\n41|\n42|// statOpenedFile stats an already-opened descriptor. It is a variable\n43|// only so seam-failure tests can force the stat-failure path through the\n44|// production openers below: fstat on a live descriptor does not fail on\n45|// any supported platform, and without the seam the error branch is\n46|// untestable. Production code never reassigns it.\n47|var statOpenedFile = func(file *os.File) (os.FileInfo, error) {\n48|\treturn file.Stat()\n49|}\n50|\n51|// OpenNoFollowDir opens an existing directory read-only without following\n52|// a trailing symlink, returning a handle for validation walks. The handle\n53|// pins the opened directory: Fstat and ReadDirnames on it observe the\n54|// directory that was opened even if its name is later retargeted.\n55|//\n56|// The Lstat fast path refuses non-directories with the same rules on\n57|// every platform; the post-open Stat below stays authoritative against a\n58|// swap between the check and the open.\n59|func OpenNoFollowDir(path string) (*os.File, error) {\n60|\tif info, err := os.Lstat(path); err != nil {\n61|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n62|\t} else if info.Mode()&os.ModeSymlink != 0 {\n63|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"target symlink\", info.Name())\n64|\t} else if !info.IsDir() {\n65|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"target directory\", info.Name())\n66|\t}\n67|\tdir, err := openNoFollowDir(path)\n68|\tif err != nil {\n69|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n70|\t}\n71|\tinfo, err := statOpenedFile(dir)\n72|\tif err != nil {\n73|\t\t_ = dir.Close()\n74|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open stat failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n75|\t}\n76|\tif info == nil || !info.IsDir() || info.Mode()&os.ModeSymlink != 0 {\n77|\t\t_ = dir.Close()\n78|\t\tname := path\n79|\t\tif info != nil {\n80|\t\t\tname = info.Name()\n81|\t\t}\n82|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"target directory\", name)\n83|\t}\n84|\treturn dir, nil\n85|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c429","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07721-ca06-7081-babf-b563190d8d78"},"sequence":111,"recorded_at":1780531400000217,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd"},"call_id":"call_01a07721ffc17711bdee7ebf56a4b682","text":"Read text file `internal/secprim/path.go`.\n1|package secprim\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"os\"\n5|\t\"path\"\n6|\t\"strings\"\n7|\n8|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/scalar\"\n9|)\n10|\n11|// CheckMemberPath admits one staging or archive member name on the given\n12|// platform under Section 16.3. The member is a slash-separated relative\n13|// path: the shared relative grammar (absolute, drive-qualified, backslash,\n14|// NUL, empty, dot, parent segments, and repeatedly encoded separators) is\n15|// decided by scalar.ParseRelativePath, which owns it. This gate adds only\n16|// the platform rules scalar does not own because they apply to member\n17|// names rather than to absolute paths:\n18|//\n19|//   - encoded dots and overlong separators (%2e, %c0%af, any case and %25\n20|//     nesting): an extension past scalar, because extraction stacks\n21|//     commonly percent-decode after validation and an admitted \"%2e%2e\"\n22|//     becomes a parent segment below this gate;\n23|//   - Windows alternate-stream and NTFS syntax: any colon outside a refused\n24|//     drive prefix (Section 16.3 \"alternate streams\");\n25|//   - Windows trailing dots and spaces per segment, which the filesystem\n26|//     strips and which therefore alias distinct members;\n27|//   - Windows reserved device names, decided by the same table scalar uses\n28|//     for absolute paths.\n29|func CheckMemberPath(platform scalar.Platform, member string) error {\n30|\tif _, err := scalar.ParseRelativePath(member); err != nil {\n31|\t\treturn failPath(\"member grammar\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n32|\t}\n33|\tif containsEncodedDot(member) {\n34|\t\treturn failPath(\"member encoded dot\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n35|\t}\n36|\tif containsOverlongSeparator(member) {\n37|\t\treturn failPath(\"member overlong separator\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n38|\t}\n39|\tif platform == scalar.PlatformWindows {\n40|\t\tfor _, segment := range strings.Split(member, \"/\") {\n41|\t\t\tif strings.Contains(segment, \":\") {\n42|\t\t\t\treturn failPath(\"member alternate stream\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n43|\t\t\t}\n44|\t\t\tif strings.HasSuffix(segment, \".\") || strings.HasSuffix(segment, \" \") {\n45|\t\t\t\treturn failPath(\"member trailing dot or space\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n46|\t\t\t}\n47|\t\t\tif scalar.IsReservedWindowsDeviceName(segment) {\n48|\t\t\t\treturn failPath(\"member reserved device name\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n49|\t\t\t}\n50|\t\t}\n51|\t}\n52|\treturn nil\n53|}\n54|\n55|// unwrapPercentEncoding folds %25 layers the way scalar's separator check\n56|// does, so a twice-encoded dot or separator cannot hide behind its outer\n57|// encoding.\n58|func unwrapPercentEncoding(value string) string {\n59|\tcandidate := strings.ToLower(value)\n60|\tfor {\n61|\t\tnext := strings.ReplaceAll(candidate, \"%25\", \"%\")\n62|\t\tif next == candidate {\n63|\t\t\treturn candidate\n64|\t\t}\n65|\t\tcandidate = next\n66|\t}\n67|}\n68|\n69|// containsEncodedDot reports a percent-encoded dot (%2e, any case, any\n70|// %25 nesting). scalar owns the encoded-separator rule (%2f/%5c) but not\n71|// encoded dots; this gate extends it because extraction stacks commonly\n72|// percent-decode after validation, turning an admitted \"%2e%2e\" into a\n73|// parent segment below this gate. A literal percent followed by other\n74|// hex (for example \"100%.txt\" or \"a%20b\") keeps passing through.\n75|func containsEncodedDot(member string) bool {\n76|\treturn strings.Contains(unwrapPercentEncoding(member), \"%2e\")\n77|}\n78|\n79|// containsOverlongSeparator reports the overlong UTF-8 encoding of slash\n80|// (%c0%af, any case, any %25 nesting), which some decoders accept as a\n81|// separator. Same ownership rationale as containsEncodedDot.\n82|func containsOverlongSeparator(member string) bool {\n83|\treturn strings.Contains(unwrapPercentEncoding(member), \"%c0%af\")\n84|}\n85|\n86|// memberErrorTarget names the refused member without echoing unbounded\n87|// foreign bytes: the first 64 characters identify the site for an\n88|// operator while a hostile multi-megabyte member cannot flood a log.\n89|func memberErrorTarget(member string) string {\n90|\tif len(member) > 64 {\n91|\t\treturn member[:64] + \"...\"\n92|\t}\n93|\treturn member\n94|}\n95|\n96|// Guard is a containment boundary for staging and extraction: a validated\n97|// absolute root on one platform plus the optional exact set of managed\n98|// member names that may be replaced. A nil managed set disables the\n99|// managed check; a non-nil set (even an empty one) restricts Resolve to\n100|// its members, so denying by default is representable and distinct from\n101|// not checking. The zero value is unusable; build one with NewGuard.\n102|type Guard struct {\n103|\troot         string\n104|\tplatform     scalar.Platform\n105|\tmanaged      map[string]bool\n106|\tcheckManaged bool\n107|}\n108|\n109|// NewGuard validates the staging root and records the managed set.\n110|// The root must be an absolute path on the platform: a relative root\n111|// fails here instead of resolving against the process working directory\n112|// at commit time.\n113|func NewGuard(root string, platform scalar.Platform, managed []string) (Guard, error) {\n114|\tif _, err := scalar.ParseAbsolutePath(platform, root); err != nil {\n115|\t\treturn Guard{}, failPath(\"guard root\", memberErrorTarget(root))\n116|\t}\n117|\tguard := Guard{root: root, platform: platform}\n118|\tif managed != nil {\n119|\t\tguard.checkManaged = true\n120|\t\tguard.managed = make(map[string]bool, len(managed))\n121|\t\tfor _, member := range managed {\n122|\t\t\tguard.managed[member] = true\n123|\t\t}\n124|\t}\n125|\treturn guard, nil\n126|}\n127|\n128|// Resolve maps one member name to its commit path inside the guard root.\n129|// It enforces the two conjuncts of containment independently: the member\n130|// grammar (CheckMemberPath, which refuses traversal lexically) and the\n131|// joined-prefix check below (which refuses anything whose cleaned join\n132|// escapes the root even if the grammar ever admits a new shape). Either\n133|// conjunct refuses alone.\n134|//\n135|// The prefix check is currently unreachable through this entry: the\n136|// member grammar refuses every parent segment, so no admitted member can\n137|// produce a join outside the root. It stays as defense in depth against a\n138|// future grammar relaxation, and TestGuardPrefixCheckIsLoadBearing pins\n139|// the property it guards (every resolved join stays within the root) so\n140|// weakening the grammar reddens there first.\n141|func (guard Guard) Resolve(member string) (string, error) {\n142|\tif err := CheckMemberPath(guard.platform, member); err != nil {\n143|\t\treturn \"\", err\n144|\t}\n145|\tif guard.checkManaged && !guard.managed[member] {\n146|\t\treturn \"\", failPath(\"member unmanaged\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n147|\t}\n148|\tjoined := joinSlash(guard.slashRoot(), member)\n149|\tif !withinRoot(guard.slashRoot(), joined) {\n150|\t\treturn \"\", failPath(\"member containment\", memberErrorTarget(member))\n151|\t}\n152|\tif guard.platform == scalar.PlatformWindows {\n153|\t\treturn strings.ReplaceAll(joined, \"/\", `\\`), nil\n154|\t}\n155|\treturn joined, nil\n156|}\n157|\n158|// slashRoot renders the validated root with forward slashes so the join\n159|// and prefix check are platform-parameterized rather than host-native:\n160|// windows cases are decided lexically on any host.\n161|func (guard Guard) slashRoot() string {\n162|\tif guard.platform == scalar.PlatformWindows {\n163|\t\treturn strings.ReplaceAll(guard.root, `\\`, \"/\")\n164|\t}\n165|\treturn guard.root\n166|}\n167|\n168|// joinSlash joins a slash-form root with a grammar-checked member and\n169|// cleans the result, preserving a UNC leading double slash that path\n170|// Clean would otherwise collapse.\n171|func joinSlash(slashRoot, member string) string {\n172|\tfull := strings.TrimSuffix(slashRoot, \"/\") + \"/\" + member\n173|\tcleaned := path.Clean(full)\n174|\tif strings.HasPrefix(slashRoot, \"//\") && !strings.HasPrefix(cleaned, \"//\") {\n175|\t\tcleaned = \"/\" + cleaned\n176|\t}\n177|\treturn cleaned\n178|}\n179|\n180|// withinRoot reports whether the cleaned join stays inside the cleaned\n181|// root: equality (the root itself) or a slash-boundary prefix. A bare\n182|// string prefix is not containment: \"/root-evil\" starts with \"/root\" but\n183|// is not inside it.\n184|func withinRoot(slashRoot, joined string) bool {\n185|\tbase := strings.TrimSuffix(slashRoot, \"/\")\n186|\tif base == \"\" {\n187|\t\tbase = \"/\"\n188|\t}\n189|\tif joined == base {\n190|\t\treturn true\n191|\t}\n192|\treturn strings.HasPrefix(joined, base+\"/\")\n193|}\n194|\n195|// DetectCaseCollision reports whether candidate collides with an existing\n196|// member name under case folding for a case-insensitive destination\n197|// (Section 16.3 \"case-fold collisions\"). It returns the colliding entry.\n198|// Comparison is Unicode simple folding via EqualFold over the exact entry\n199|// spellings the caller lists: the caller owns the directory read, this\n200|// function owns the decision.\n201|func DetectCaseCollision(seen []string, candidate string) (string, bool) {\n202|\tfor _, existing := range seen {\n203|\t\tif existing != candidate && strings.EqualFold(existing, candidate) {\n204|\t\t\treturn existing, true\n205|\t\t}\n206|\t}\n207|\treturn \"\", false\n208|}\n209|\n210|// CheckRegularTarget refuses a stat result that is not a plain regular\n211|// file: symlinks, directories, devices, named pipes, and sockets\n212|// (Section 16.3 \"symlink/reparse escape\" and \"device/FIFO/socket\n213|// creation\"). A nil result is refused rather than treated as absent: a\n214|// failed stat is never a legitimate absence.\n215|func CheckRegularTarget(info os.FileInfo) error {\n216|\tif info == nil {\n217|\t\treturn failPath(\"target stat missing\", \"nil file info\")\n218|\t}\n219|\tmode := info.Mode()\n220|\tswitch {\n221|\tcase mode&os.ModeSymlink != 0:\n222|\t\treturn failPath(\"target symlink\", info.Name())\n223|\tcase info.IsDir():\n224|\t\treturn failPath(\"target directory\", info.Name())\n225|\tcase mode&os.ModeDevice != 0,\n226|\t\tmode&os.ModeNamedPipe != 0,\n227|\t\tmode&os.ModeSocket != 0,\n228|\t\tmode&os.ModeCharDevice != 0,\n229|\t\t!mode.IsRegular():\n230|\t\treturn failPath(\"target special file\", info.Name())\n231|\t}\n232|\treturn nil\n233|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c42b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07721-ca06-7081-babf-b563190d8d78"},"sequence":112,"recorded_at":1780531400000219,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd"},"call_id":"call_01a07722006b725099650a1faf59d294","text":"Read text file `internal/secprim/open_nofollow_unix.go`.\n1|//go:build !windows\n2|\n3|package secprim\n4|\n5|import (\n6|\t\"os\"\n7|\n8|\t\"golang.org/x/sys/unix\"\n9|)\n10|\n11|// openNoFollow opens path read-only without following a trailing symlink:\n12|// O_NOFOLLOW makes the open itself fail on a symlink target instead of\n13|// traversing it. O_CLOEXEC keeps the descriptor out of any spawned child.\n14|func openNoFollow(path string) (*os.File, error) {\n15|\treturn os.OpenFile(path, os.O_RDONLY|unix.O_NOFOLLOW|unix.O_CLOEXEC, 0)\n16|}\n17|\n18|// openNoFollowDir opens a directory read-only without following a trailing\n19|// symlink, returning a handle that pins the directory for validation walks\n20|// (Fstat, ReadDirnames) instead of re-resolving its name.\n21|func openNoFollowDir(path string) (*os.File, error) {\n22|\treturn os.OpenFile(path, os.O_RDONLY|unix.O_NOFOLLOW|unix.O_CLOEXEC|unix.O_DIRECTORY, 0)\n23|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c42d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07721-ca06-7081-babf-b563190d8d78"},"sequence":113,"recorded_at":1780531400000221,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd"},"call_id":"call_01a0772200f97c1188d471dd740c3aca","text":"Read text file `internal/secprim/env.go`.\n1|package secprim\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"sort\"\n5|\t\"strings\"\n6|\t\"unicode/utf8\"\n7|)\n8|\n9|// IsEnvName reports whether name satisfies the environment-name grammar\n10|// shared by launch allowlists and SpawnPlan admission:\n11|// [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,127}. internal/provhost delegates its wire-plan\n12|// name check to this function, so the two packages cannot drift on what a\n13|// name is; the byte-counted plan bounds around it stay in provhost.\n14|func IsEnvName(name string) bool {\n15|\tif len(name) < 1 || len(name) > 128 {\n16|\t\treturn false\n17|\t}\n18|\tfor index := 0; index < len(name); index++ {\n19|\t\tchar := name[index]\n20|\t\tvalid := char == '_' ||\n21|\t\t\tchar >= 'A' && char <= 'Z' ||\n22|\t\t\tchar >= 'a' && char <= 'z' ||\n23|\t\t\tindex > 0 && char >= '0' && char <= '9'\n24|\t\tif !valid {\n25|\t\t\treturn false\n26|\t\t}\n27|\t}\n28|\treturn true\n29|}\n30|\n31|// BuildEnv builds the exact environment of a child process from a minimal\n32|// allowlist plus operator literals (Section 16.7 \"minimal environment\n33|// allowlists\"):\n34|//\n35|//   - allowed names the inherited variables the child may see. Each must\n36|//     satisfy IsEnvName; duplicates are refused rather than silently\n37|//     collapsed, because a duplicated allowlist entry is a configuration\n38|//     error. A name absent from lookup is skipped, not failed: an unset\n39|//     variable is a legitimate absence, while a failed read must never\n40|//     masquerade as one, so lookup reports only what it read.\n41|//   - literals carries explicit key=value pairs (workspace-derived values,\n42|//     SpawnPlan literals). Keys must satisfy IsEnvName and must be disjoint\n43|//     from allowed: a literal that shadows an inherited name would make the\n44|//     child's environment depend on map order rather than on policy.\n45|//   - values must be NUL-free valid UTF-8. Empty values are admitted:\n46|//     FOO= is a meaningful empty assignment, not a missing one.\n47|//\n48|// The result is sorted by name and rendered k=v, so identical inputs build\n49|// byte-identical environments. lookup abstracts the parent environment for\n50|// tests; production passes os.LookupEnv.\n51|//\n52|// No length bound is enforced here: the Section 5.1 literal bounds apply\n53|// to the SpawnPlan wire object in internal/provhost. No default allowlist\n54|// is declared here either: with no provider implementation in this\n55|// repository to derive runtime needs from, a baked-in list would be an\n56|// invented product policy, so the operator supplies the list explicitly\n57|// and ExecRunner inherits the parent environment until one is supplied.\n58|func BuildEnv(allowed []string, literals map[string]string, lookup func(string) (string, bool)) ([]string, error) {\n59|\tif lookup == nil {\n60|\t\treturn nil, failEnv(\"env lookup missing\", \"no parent environment reader\")\n61|\t}\n62|\tseen := make(map[string]bool, len(allowed))\n63|\tfor _, name := range allowed {\n64|\t\tif !IsEnvName(name) {\n65|\t\t\treturn nil, failEnv(\"env name\", name)\n66|\t\t}\n67|\t\tif seen[name] {\n68|\t\t\treturn nil, failEnv(\"env allowlist duplicate\", name)\n69|\t\t}\n70|\t\tseen[name] = true\n71|\t}\n72|\tfor key := range literals {\n73|\t\tif !IsEnvName(key) {\n74|\t\t\treturn nil, failEnv(\"env name\", key)\n75|\t\t}\n76|\t\tif seen[key] {\n77|\t\t\treturn nil, failEnv(\"env literal collision\", key)\n78|\t\t}\n79|\t}\n80|\tvalues := make(map[string]string, len(allowed)+len(literals))\n81|\tfor _, name := range allowed {\n82|\t\tvalue, present := lookup(name)\n83|\t\tif !present {\n84|\t\t\tcontinue\n85|\t\t}\n86|\t\tif strings.ContainsRune(value, 0) {\n87|\t\t\treturn nil, failEnv(\"env value NUL\", name)\n88|\t\t}\n89|\t\tif !utf8.ValidString(value) {\n90|\t\t\treturn nil, failEnv(\"env value encoding\", name)\n91|\t\t}\n92|\t\tvalues[name] = value\n93|\t}\n94|\tfor key, value := range literals {\n95|\t\tif strings.ContainsRune(value, 0) {\n96|\t\t\treturn nil, failEnv(\"env value NUL\", key)\n97|\t\t}\n98|\t\tif !utf8.ValidString(value) {\n99|\t\t\treturn nil, failEnv(\"env value encoding\", key)\n100|\t\t}\n101|\t\tvalues[key] = value\n102|\t}\n103|\tnames := make([]string, 0, len(values))\n104|\tfor name := range values {\n105|\t\tnames = append(names, name)\n106|\t}\n107|\tsort.Strings(names)\n108|\tenvironment := make([]string, 0, len(names))\n109|\tfor _, name := range names {\n110|\t\tenvironment = append(environment, name+\"=\"+values[name])\n111|\t}\n112|\treturn environment, nil\n113|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c42f","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07721-ca06-7081-babf-b563190d8d78"},"sequence":114,"recorded_at":1780531400000223,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd"},"call_id":"call_01a0772201aa71709b181c09140e0b51","text":"Read text file `internal/secprim/render.go`.\n1|package secprim\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"strings\"\n5|)\n6|\n7|// Escape control symbols rendered visibly.\n8|const (\n9|\t// escapeSymbol replaces ESC so ANSI CSI/OSC sequences become inert yet\n10|\t// visible: the byte the terminal would interpret is gone, and the\n11|\t// operator can still see that something was neutralized.\n12|\tescapeSymbol = \"␛\" // U+241B SYMBOL FOR ESCAPE\n13|\t// neutralizedSymbol replaces every other refused control: C0/C1\n14|\t// controls, DEL, bidi overrides, and zero-width format characters.\n15|\tneutralizedSymbol = \"�\" // U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER\n16|)\n17|\n18|// EscapeForTerminal renders untrusted text inert for terminal output\n19|// (Section 16.7: TUI and CLI rendering neutralizes ANSI/OSC sequences,\n20|// control characters, bidi overrides, invalid encodings, and hostile-width\n21|// graphemes before terminal output). The rule per rune:\n22|//\n23|//   - '\\n' and '\\t' pass through: multiline human output needs them, and\n24|//     neither starts an escape sequence or reorders text;\n25|//   - ESC becomes U+241B, so CSI, OSC, and every other ESC-led sequence\n26|//     loses its introducer while staying visible;\n27|//   - every other C0 control, DEL, and every C1 control (including the\n28|//     single-byte CSI U+009B) becomes U+FFFD;\n29|//   - bidi overrides and isolates (U+202A-U+202E, U+2066-U+2069), the\n30|//     explicit directional marks (U+200E, U+200F, U+061C), and the\n31|//     zero-width characters (U+200B-U+200D, U+FEFF) become U+FFFD: they\n32|//     reorder or hide text without showing anything;\n33|//   - invalid UTF-8 becomes U+FFFD per run, so the result is always valid\n34|//     UTF-8 and no raw byte reaches the terminal;\n35|//   - every other rune passes through unchanged.\n36|//\n37|// The function is idempotent: every symbol it emits is a fixed point, so\n38|// escaping already-escaped output changes nothing and double-wiring a\n39|// render path cannot corrupt text.\n40|//\n41|// Stated bounds. Soft hyphen (U+00AD), combining-mark floods, emoji\n42|// presentation sequences, and other grapheme-width anomalies are not\n43|// measured: neutralizing them needs terminal width tables this package\n44|// does not carry, and stripping combining marks would mangle legitimate\n45|// non-Latin text. Full-width spoofing beyond the enumerated invisible\n46|// controls is therefore unproven and named here rather than claimed.\n47|func EscapeForTerminal(text string) string {\n48|\ttext = strings.ToValidUTF8(text, neutralizedSymbol)\n49|\tvar rendered strings.Builder\n50|\trendered.Grow(len(text))\n51|\tfor _, character := range text {\n52|\t\tswitch {\n53|\t\tcase character == '\\n' || character == '\\t':\n54|\t\t\trendered.WriteRune(character)\n55|\t\tcase character == 0x1b:\n56|\t\t\trendered.WriteString(escapeSymbol)\n57|\t\tcase character < 0x20 || character == 0x7f || character >= 0x80 && character <= 0x9f:\n58|\t\t\trendered.WriteString(neutralizedSymbol)\n59|\t\tcase character >= 0x202a && character <= 0x202e,\n60|\t\t\tcharacter >= 0x2066 && character <= 0x2069,\n61|\t\t\tcharacter == 0x200e || character == 0x200f || character == 0x061c,\n62|\t\t\tcharacter >= 0x200b && character <= 0x200d,\n63|\t\t\tcharacter == 0xfeff:\n64|\t\t\trendered.WriteString(neutralizedSymbol)\n65|\t\tdefault:\n66|\t\t\trendered.WriteRune(character)\n67|\t\t}\n68|\t}\n69|\treturn rendered.String()\n70|}\n71|\n72|// RenderForTerminal prepares one untrusted line for terminal display:\n73|// secret scrubbing first (so a secret containing an escape sequence is\n74|// removed as a secret, not merely neutralized), then control escaping.\n75|// secrets carries the exact secret values the caller knows; nil admits no\n76|// corpus redaction while key-pattern and private-key scrubbing still\n77|// apply. The composition is idempotent because both stages are.\n78|func RenderForTerminal(line string, secrets []string) string {\n79|\treturn EscapeForTerminal(Redact(line, secrets))\n80|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
