{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c78d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07721-ca06-7081-babf-b563190d8d78"},"sequence":545,"recorded_at":1780531400001085,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"38f2a4ef-6e71-4516-af44-8dd3ca8a69cd"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"69e506b2-65bb-4edd-b15e-00782d82b441"},"task_id":"69e506b2-65bb-4edd-b15e-00782d82b441","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"69e506b2-65bb-4edd-b15e-00782d82b441","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-2ciy0s/TASK-260830-2ciy0s_review-verdict-rev1.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-2ciy0s — reviewer verdict, CR revision 1\n2|\n3|- Run: `RUN-260906-43ee79` (reviewer/reviewer)\n4|- Change Request: `CR-TASK-260830-2ciy0s-1` rev 1, `kind=task_delta`,\n5|  base `157a54c`, candidate tree `9f821f3e5602137d3c8470291394d011df1b6f05`,\n6|  `repository_delta=present`, 30 changed paths.\n7|- Verdict: **changes requested** → `to-dev`.\n8|- `repeat-of:` **none** (first round of this leaf).\n9|- Worktree verified byte-identical to the candidate tree before and after\n10|  every probe (`git read-tree HEAD` into a temp index + `git add -A` +\n11|  `write-tree` → `9f821f3e…`). All mutants and plants reverted.\n12|\n13|## What I re-ran myself (not accepted from the producer)\n14|\n15|| Gate | Command | Result |\n16|| --- | --- | ---: |\n17|| Full suite | `go test ./... -count=1` | exit 0, 20 packages ok |\n18|| Format | `gofmt -l internal/` | clean |\n19|| Vet | `go vet ./...` | exit 0 |\n20|| Windows vet | `GOOS=windows go vet ./...` | exit 0 |\n21|| Windows build | `GOOS=windows go build ./...` | exit 0 |\n22|\n23|Accepted from the producer without re-running: `-race`, `-cover`\n24|(secprim 94.5%), `tracecheck`. Everything else below I executed.\n25|\n26|## Blocking findings\n27|\n28|### B1 — Containment and no-follow do not compose: a symlinked intermediate component escapes the guard root\n29|\n30|Driven through the real entry points, no mocks:\n31|\n32|```\n33|NewGuard(\"<tmp>/stage\", PlatformLinux, nil)   -> ok\n34|os.Symlink(\"<tmp>/outside\", \"<tmp>/stage/sub\")\n35|Guard.Resolve(\"sub/loot\")                     -> \"<tmp>/stage/sub/loot\", err=nil\n36|OpenNoFollowFile(\"<tmp>/stage/sub/loot\")      -> ADMITTED, read \"ESCAPED\"\n37|```\n38|\n39|`O_NOFOLLOW` constrains the **final** component only. `Guard.Resolve` is\n40|purely lexical. Composing them therefore does not produce containment:\n41|any hostile intermediate directory that is a symlink is traversed by the\n42|open, and the read leaves the root.\n43|\n44|Section 16.3 requires refusal of \"symlink/reparse escape during **both**\n45|validation and commit\" and TOCTOU prevention \"by validating **through\n46|directory handles** or equivalent safe filesystem primitives\". The\n47|package exposes no `openat`-relative API: `OpenNoFollowDir` returns an\n48|`*os.File` that nothing in the package or the repository ever opens\n49|through. `nofollow.go` states the mitigation as \"Parent directories are\n50|the caller's responsibility: open the staging root with `OpenNoFollowDir`\n51|and resolve members through a `Guard`\" — that composition is not\n52|implementable with the shipped surface, because there is no entry point\n53|that takes the directory handle.\n54|\n55|The outcome doc lists \"symlink/reparse escape at validation and commit\"\n56|and \"TOCTOU dir-handle primitives (unix)\" among the 13 rows **\"fully\n57|driven through production entries\"**. Neither is: the property does not\n58|hold, and no test drives a symlinked parent.\n59|\n60|Named fix: add a handle-relative open (`unix.Openat` with\n61|`O_NOFOLLOW|O_CLOEXEC` per component, walking `Guard.Resolve`'s segments\n62|from an `OpenNoFollowDir` root handle), and drive the escape fixture\n63|above as a negative test. Windows keeps its stated check-then-open bound.\n64|\n65|### B2 — `OpenNoFollowFile` on a FIFO blocks forever instead of refusing\n66|\n67|```\n68|mkfifo(\"<tmp>/stage/pipe\")\n69|OpenNoFollowFile(\"<tmp>/stage/pipe\")  -> *** still blocked after 3s ***\n70|```\n71|\n72|A read-only `open(2)` on a FIFO with no writer blocks until one appears.\n73|`CheckRegularTarget` runs **after** the open and is never reached, so the\n74|\"device/FIFO/socket\" refusal that `path.go` implements is unreachable\n75|through the opener. A hostile staging tree containing one FIFO stalls the\n76|reader indefinitely.\n77|\n78|`fifo_unix_test.go` proves what it proves and no more: it builds a FIFO\n79|and feeds the resulting `os.Lstat` **info** to `CheckRegularTarget`. It\n80|never calls `OpenNoFollowFile` on the FIFO. So the pure function is\n81|covered and the production opener is not — and the production opener is\n82|where the claim is false. The outcome doc lists \"device/FIFO/socket\n83|refusal\" as fully driven through production entries; it is not driven\n84|through the opener at all.\n85|\n86|Named fix: add `unix.O_NONBLOCK` to the unix open flags (POSIX: a\n87|read-only `O_NONBLOCK` open of a FIFO returns immediately), keep the\n88|post-open `CheckRegularTarget`, clear `O_NONBLOCK` on the descriptor if\n89|the target turns out to be a regular file, and drive the FIFO through\n90|`OpenNoFollowFile` with a test that would hang without the fix.\n91|\n92|### B3 — 3 of 6 `cliresult` wiring sites are unmeasured, including the stdout success path\n93|\n94|Narrowing mutants I applied and ran (`terminalLine(x)` → `x` at one site):\n95|\n96|| Site | Mutant | Result |\n97|| --- | --- | ---: |\n98|| `Emit` failure → stderr | R22 | KILLED (`TestTextEmissionNeutralizesHostileContent`) |\n99|| `Log` / `Prompt` write | R28 | not applicable (shared literal); driven by the same test |\n100|| **`Emit` success → stdout** | **R27** | **SURVIVED** |\n101|| **`Progress`** | **R23** | **SURVIVED** |\n102|| **`Prompt` non-interactive refusal text** | **R24** | **SURVIVED** |\n103|\n104|`render_wiring_test.go` drives `Log`, interactive `Prompt`, and failure\n105|`Emit`. Success `Emit` — the primary human stdout path — `Progress`, and\n106|the `ErrPromptForbidden` message are wired but unwitnessed. The README\n107|claims escaping is \"wired into every `cliresult` text path\"; the code is\n108|right today and only half of it would redden if that stopped being true.\n109|\n110|### B4 — `ExecRunner.Env` nil-vs-empty is unmeasured, and the deny-all allowlist is the case that breaks\n111|\n112|Mutant R26: `if runner.Env != nil` → `if len(runner.Env) > 0`. **SURVIVED**\n113|the full suite.\n114|\n115|`runner.go` names this exact distinction as the design point (\"when nil\n116|the child inherits …; when non-nil the child observes exactly `Env`\"),\n117|and `env.go` makes the same argument for `Guard`'s managed set (\"denying\n118|by default is representable and distinct from not checking\"). Under the\n119|surviving mutant an operator who supplies an empty allowlist — the\n120|deny-all case, the whole point of an allowlist — silently gets full\n121|parent-environment inheritance. Add `TestExecRunnerDeliversExactlyTheBuiltEnv`\n122|sibling driving `ExecRunner{Env: []string{}}` and asserting an empty\n123|child environment.\n124|\n125|### B5 — the refusal-inventory gate is identifier-keyed; 4 of 4 planted bypasses walk through\n126|\n127|`auditRefusalInventory` derives sites by matching `call.Fun.(*ast.Ident)`\n128|against the three names `failPath`/`failArgv`/`failEnv`. Every plant below\n129|adds an **unexercised** production refusal; the control proves the\n130|instrument works.\n131|\n132|| Plant | Shape | Result |\n133|| --- | --- | ---: |\n134|| CONTROL | `failArgv(\"plant control\", …)` | **CAUGHT** — `refusal site argv.go:84 never fired` |\n135|| A | `var failArgvAlias = failArgv` then `failArgvAlias(…)` | BYPASSED |\n136|| B | `return &Error{Kind: ErrUnsafeArgv, …}` | BYPASSED |\n137|| C | `plantMaker.argv(…)` (method selector) | BYPASSED |\n138|| D | `make := failArgv` then `make(…)` | BYPASSED |\n139|\n140|So \"every refusal exit fired during the suite\" is true only of refusals\n141|spelled with the bare constructor identifier. `errors.go` states the rule\n142|(\"production code must not build `Error` values any other way\") but\n143|nothing enforces it — B is a direct, invisible violation of the stated\n144|rule. This is the same identifier-keyed-AST-gate class the previous story\n145|had to close; it is unrepaired here. Close it by typing on the resolved\n146|callee (or by keying on the returned `*Error` construction) and re-run\n147|all four plants as the acceptance evidence.\n148|\n149|### B6 — `TestEscapeForTerminalOutputBytes` asserts on 16 code points but feeds 10; six assertions are vacuous\n150|\n151|The `forbidden` loop enumerates `202A 202B 202C 202D 202E 2066 2067 2068\n152|2069 200E 200F 061C 200B 200C 200D FEFF`. The corpus it escapes contains\n153|only `202E 2066 2067 200E 200F 061C 200B 200C 200D FEFF`. The other six\n154|are asserted absent from an output they were never present in.\n155|\n156|Confirmed by narrowing mutants on `render.go`:\n157|\n158|| Mutant | Narrowed to admit | Result |\n159|| --- | --- | ---: |\n160|| R4 `< 0x20` → `< 0x1f` | U+001F | KILLED |\n161|| R5 C1 upper `<= 0x9f` → `<= 0x9e` | U+009F | KILLED |\n162|| R6 C1 lower `>= 0x80` → `> 0x80` | U+0080 | KILLED |\n163|| R8 zero-width `<= 0x200d` → `<= 0x200c` | U+200D | KILLED |\n164|| R9 override `<= 0x202e` → `<= 0x202d` | U+202E | KILLED |\n165|| R10 drop U+200F | U+200F | KILLED |\n166|| R11 `0xfeff` → `0xfefe` | U+FEFF | KILLED |\n167|| **R9b override `>= 0x202a` → `>= 0x202b`** | **U+202A** | **SURVIVED** |\n168|| **R7 isolate `<= 0x2069` → `<= 0x2068`** | **U+2069** | **SURVIVED** |\n169|| **R7c isolate `<= 0x2069` → `<= 0x2067`** | **U+2068, U+2069** | **SURVIVED** |\n170|\n171|Each bidi range is witnessed at exactly one endpoint. U+202A–U+202D (four\n172|fifths of the Trojan-Source embedding/override set) and U+2068–U+2069 are\n173|unmeasured. Fix is one line: put all sixteen runes in the corpus.\n174|\n175|### B7 — `TestNoArchiveEmissionPath` never scans nested production packages, and it is the sole witness for five `nopath` dispositions\n176|\n177|The walk reads `internal/<pkg>/*.go` one level deep and `continue`s on any\n178|`entry.IsDir()`. This repository already contains nested production\n179|packages (`internal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen`, `internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck`).\n180|\n181|| Plant | Location | Result |\n182|| --- | --- | ---: |\n183|| CONTROL | `import \"archive/zip\"` in `internal/scalar/path.go` | **CAUGHT** |\n184|| NESTED | identical import in `internal/catalog/cmd/cataloggen/main.go` | **BYPASSED** |\n185|\n186|`classes_test.go` cites this test as the proof for the `nopath`\n187|disposition of five of the nine Section 16.2 classes (Machine\n188|authentication, Live process identity, IPC/runtime, Transient locking,\n189|Mutable derived indexes). Beyond the directory blind spot, what it\n190|actually establishes is narrower than what the rows claim: it proves that\n191|no scanned file imports `archive/tar`, `archive/zip`, or `compress/gzip`.\n192|Section 16.2 governs \"manifest **or bundle**\" emission, and a JSON\n193|manifest needs none of those imports. Recursive-walk the tree, and state\n194|the row as the bound it is (\"no archive-format emission path\") rather\n195|than as \"no in-repo emission path carries this class\".\n196|\n197|### B8 — the mutant battery is mostly deletions, and its denominator is 6 of 35\n198|\n199|Re-derived from production with the same AST shape the inventory gate\n200|uses: `internal/secprim` has **35 refusal exits** (argv 5, env 9,\n201|nofollow 8, path 13). Mapping the producer's table onto them, the battery\n202|narrows six — M1 (`member encoded dot`), M2 (`member alternate stream`),\n203|M3 (`member reserved device name`), M4 (`argv NUL`), M5 (`env literal\n204|collision`), M10 (`member unmanaged`) — i.e. **6 of 35 = 17%**. M6–M9 hit\n205|the render/redact arms, M11/M12/M15 mutate **test instruments** rather\n206|than production gates, M13b/M14 hit wiring.\n207|\n208|Shape: twelve of the fifteen rows are arm deletions written in the\n209|\"narrows the gate to\" column. \"Drop the encoded-dot arm\" admits the whole\n210|encoded-dot class, not one member of it; that is a delete mutant with a\n211|narrowing label. Only M9 (widening the PEM match) and M11\n212|(token-preserving `\"s\"+\"h\"`) are genuinely non-deletion. Per the DoD row,\n213|a delete-only mutant proves the gate exists and says nothing about the\n214|class boundary.\n215|\n216|M13a is correctly reported as a separate COMPILE_FAIL row and is not\n217|folded into the kill count — that part is honest.\n218|\n219|I ran 20 additional mutants against the unmeasured majority. Fourteen\n220|were killed, confirming the bounds below are real: `IsEnvName` length at\n221|exactly 128/129 (R1) and its leading-digit guard (R2), `minSecretRunes`\n222|at exactly 8 (R3), the `%25`-unwrap on both the encoded-dot and overlong\n223|arms (R13/R14), the Windows trailing-space arm independently of the\n224|trailing-dot arm (R15), `EqualFold` vs `==` in case-collision (R16), the\n225|argv UTF-8 and empty-element arms (R17/R18), the PEM `PRIVATE KEY` suffix\n226|requirement (R19), URL userinfo (R20), the corpus arm (R21), the\n227|`ExecRunner` argv gate (R25b), and the four render mutants above. Five\n228|survived and are B3/B4/B6. One (R12, dropping the slash boundary from\n229|`withinRoot`) survived consistently with the producer's own declared\n230|`defensiveSites` bound — that one is not a finding.\n231|\n232|## What passed, and passed well\n233|\n234|- **G-D, no third trust path: confirmed.** I read both cited call sites.\n235|  `provider.trustCandidate` does `Canonicalize` → `Inspect` → `IsRegular`\n236|  → `owner.Approves(info.UID)` → digest; `terminalbackend.DigestFile`\n237|  does `EvalSymlinks` → `Stat` → `IsRegular` → digest with **no owner\n238|  fact**. The Section 7.1 / Section 4.B difference the doc cites is real\n239|  and visible in the code, not asserted. `secprim` adds no canonicalise,\n240|  no digest, and no owner check — there is no third path.\n241|- **The Section 16.2 roster derivation is real and fails closed.** It\n242|  parses the Class table out of the pinned `SPEC.md`, `t.Fatal`s on zero\n243|  derived classes, and checks both directions against the disposition\n244|  rows. Nine derived, nine dispositioned.\n245|- **The secret-site census fails closed for its vocabulary.** Control\n246|  plant `var PlantApiKeyStore = \"sk-live-…\"` in production →\n247|  `derived secret sites with no disposition row: secprim|decl|PlantApiKeyStore`.\n248|  Plants outside the 13-token vocabulary (`PlantGithubPAT`,\n249|  `PlantBearerMaterial`, `PlantKeychainItem`) are not rostered — that is\n250|  inherent to a token instrument, but see the note below.\n251|- **The `Redact` shapes the spawn brief flagged are all inside stated\n252|  bounds.** `GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_…` and `{\"api_secret\":\"…\"}` are compound\n253|  keys; `redact.go` states exactly that residue (\"Compound keys outside\n254|  this list (for example `db_password`) are not matched\"), and the\n255|  exact-match-not-substring rationale is sound (substring matching would\n256|  redact `token_count`). `[REDACTED]` is a bare credential with\n257|  no key shape — also stated, and it is a key *identifier*, not the\n258|  secret. Section 16.2 itself disclaims reliable content-level scrubbing\n259|  and the function claims no more. The asymmetry the brief noticed\n260|  (`password=` caught, `GITHUB_TOKEN=` not) is explained by the table,\n261|  not by a hole in the detector. Advisory only: since `provhost` env\n262|  names are `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,127}`, a suffix rule on\n263|  `_token`/`_key`/`_secret`/`_password` would cover the dominant\n264|  environment-secret spelling at near-zero false-positive cost.\n265|- Suite, format, vet, Windows vet and Windows build all green under my\n266|  own execution.\n267|\n268|## Bounds I am recording as unknown rather than inferring\n269|\n270|- **Windows is asserted, not executed.** `open_nofollow_windows.go` is\n271|  proven to compile and vet under `GOOS=windows` and nothing more. No\n272|  Windows behavior in this CR has been executed anywhere.\n273|  `priv_windows_test.go` closes none of it — it supplies a\n274|  `privilegedUser()` returning `false` and a `makeFifo` that skips. The\n275|  check-then-open TOCTOU bound is stated honestly in the source.\n276|  Separately: the Windows opener refuses only `os.ModeSymlink`, while\n277|  Section 16.3 says \"symlink/**reparse** escape\". Whether Go's\n278|  `os.Lstat` sets `ModeSymlink` for every Windows reparse point\n279|  (junctions, mount points, AppExecLink, cloud placeholders) is **not\n280|  established** by any evidence here and I did not infer it — it needs a\n281|  Windows run or a cited Go guarantee.\n282|- **The census blind spot is wider than the row that states it.**\n283|  Residue 4 and `TestSecretScanBlindSpot` state the blind spot as\n284|  \"`auth`-substring declarations\". The instrument is in fact blind to\n285|  every name outside its 13 tokens, demonstrated above with `ghp_`, JWT,\n286|  and keychain plants. That matters because the **Machine\n287|  authentication** `nopath` row cites `TestSecretSiteRosterIsComplete` as\n288|  proving \"no secret-named field exists outside the dispositioned\n289|  tables\"; it proves \"no field whose name contains one of 13 tokens\".\n290|  Restate the row to the bound the witness actually supports.\n291|\n292|## AC-row coverage, as measured\n293|\n294|The producer reports 16 of 25 Section 16 MUST rows driven. Three of the\n295|thirteen rows claimed \"fully driven through production entries\" do not\n296|survive execution: symlink/reparse escape at commit (B1), TOCTOU\n297|dir-handle primitives (B1 — the handle is returned and never used), and\n298|device/FIFO/socket refusal (B2 — driven through `CheckRegularTarget`,\n299|never through the opener, and false there). Corrected: **13 of 25 driven,\n300|3 claimed-but-falsified, 9 bounds/pre-existing.**\n301|\n302|## Required for revision 2\n303|\n304|1. B1 — handle-relative no-follow walk so containment and no-follow\n305|   compose; negative test on the symlinked-parent escape fixture.\n306|2. B2 — `O_NONBLOCK` on the unix open; FIFO driven through\n307|   `OpenNoFollowFile`.\n308|3. B3 — witness the three unmeasured `cliresult` sites (success `Emit`,\n309|   `Progress`, `Prompt` refusal).\n310|4. B4 — witness `ExecRunner{Env: []string{}}` as deny-all.\n311|5. B5 — close the identifier-keyed inventory gate; re-run all four\n312|   plants plus the control as acceptance evidence.\n313|6. B6 — feed all sixteen enumerated runes into the escape corpus.\n314|7. B7 — recurse the emission-path walk; restate the five `nopath` rows to\n315|   the bound the witness supports.\n316|8. B8 — report the mutant denominator against the 35 derived refusal\n317|   exits, and replace delete-labelled-as-narrowing rows with real\n318|   boundary moves.\n319|\n320|Correct the outcome doc and the README to match, in particular\n321|\"fully driven through production entries\" and \"wired into every\n322|`cliresult` text path\"."}}}
